    "evidence_text": "Leptin treatment ameliorates anxiety in ob/ob obese mice.\n\nWe investigated whether or not administered leptin influences anxiety-like behavior in ob/ob mice.\n\nRepeated intraperitoneal administrations of leptin were continued for 5 days.\n\nAnxiety was assessed in the standard elevated plus maze.\n\nBody weight was measured daily.\n\n\u03b2-Adrenergic receptor antagonism prevents anxiety-like behavior and microglial reactivity induced by repeated social defeat.\n\nPsychosocial stress is associated with altered immune function and development of psychological disorders including anxiety and depression.\n\nDown-regulation of NEP could predispose to AD.\n\nOn contrary in astrocytes hypoxia increased NEP mRNA expression.\n\nBehavioral characterization of neuropeptide Y knockout mice.\n\nThe data from these studies provide further evidence of involvement of NPY in energy balance, anxiety, and possibly nociception.\n\nThe importance of neuropeptides in the hypothalamus has been experimentally established.\n\nThus, glutamate release from VMH neurons is an important component of the neurocircuitry that functions to prevent hypoglycemia.\n\nA KEY ROLE FOR OREXIN IN PANIC ANXIETY\n\nPanic disorder is a severe anxiety disorder with recurrent, debilitating panic attacks.\n\nMoreover, we show that human subjects with panic anxiety have elevated levels of ORX in the cerebrospinal fluid compared to subjects without panic anxiety.\n\nModulation of anxiety through blockade of anandamide hydrolysis\n\nThese effects are accompanied by augmented brain levels of anandamide and are prevented by CB1 receptor blockade.\n\nAgouti-related peptide\u2013expressing neurons are mandatory for feeding\n\nNevertheless, inactivation of the genes encoding NPY and/or AgRP has no impact on food intake in mice.\n\nGDX rats exhibited significantly more anxiety-like behaviors than intact or DHT-replaced rats.\n\nHowever, flutamide alone did not increase anxiety-like behavior of GDX rats.\n\nEnhancement of anxiety-like responsiveness to the cannabinoid CB(1) receptor agonist HU-210 following chronic stress.\n\nFurther, in stressed animals both the low and the high doses of HU-210 induced anxiogenic responses.\n\nThese findings suggest that chronic stress enhances either cannabinoid receptor responsivity or one of the interacting systems implicated in emotional states.",
